> Module Hoh_qss_persons

> Using Types
> Using Qualifying_child as Q
> Using Qualifying_relative as R
> Using Dependents_benefit_split as B

# Qualifying persons for head of household and qualifying surviving spouse: IRC 2(a), 2(b), 7703(b)

Direct File ("DF" below) facts `/familyAndHousehold/*/hohQualifyingPerson`
(`familyAndHousehold.xml` line 3332), `/familyAndHousehold/*/qssQualifyingPerson`
(line 3443), their helpers `qssPersonResidencyTest` (line 1886),
`flowAddPersonKeepingUpHome` (line 1903), `flowAddPersonKeepingUpParentsHome`
(line 1959), `personQualifiesTPforHoH` and `personQualifiesTPforQss` (lines
2228-2253), `isBiologicalAdoptedFosterOrStepchild` (line 4984), the household
facts `/hasHohQualifyingParent`, `/hasHohQualifyingChild` and
`/knockoutHohQpHasNoTin` (lines 4908, 5025, 5107), and
`/familyAndHousehold/*/marriedHohRelationshipTest` in
`dependentsRelationship.xml` (line 1275), under
`../direct-file/direct-file/backend/src/main/resources/tax/`. Issue #19,
scope.md section 5.3. Tests: `catala/tests/test_hoh_qss_persons.catala_en`.
Oracle comparison: `difftest/mappings/hoh_qss_persons.mjs` against the
per-person oracle `oracle/persons.csv` (`make difftest-persons`).

The file is named after the topic, as `qualifying_child.catala_en`,
`qualifying_relative.catala_en` and `dependents_benefit_split.catala_en` are
(CONVENTIONS section 9 lists `filing_status.catala_en` for #19 and #20
together; the filing-status choice itself, `/eligibleForHoh` and
`/eligibleForQss`, is issue #20 and reads the counts below).

Like the other household modules, this module works on one member of the DF
collection `/familyAndHousehold` at a time (scope `HohQssPersonTest`) and maps
that scope over the household (scope `HohQssPersons`). It calls
`Qualifying_relative.DependentTest` for the same person, which itself calls
`Qualifying_child.QualifyingChildTest` and
`Dependents_benefit_split.BenefitSplitTest`; so the qualifying child,
qualifying relative, dependent and benefit-split results used below are those
modules' outputs, not inputs.

Facts read from outside the person, all inputs here (CONVENTIONS section 7,
stage 1):

- `/minimumFilerDateOfBirth`, `/treatFilersAsDependents`,
  `/eitherFilerUSCitizenOrNational`, `/treatAsMFJ` (`Filers`, #15), passed
  to `DependentTest`;
- `/eligibleForMFJ` (`filingStatus.xml` line 512; `Filers.eligible_for_mfj`):
  the filer is married at the end of the year and not treated as unmarried by
  IRC 2(b)(2), so HoH needs IRC 7703(b);
- `/filerCouldQualifyForHoh`, `/filerCouldQualifyForQss` (`filers.xml`;
  `Filers.filer_could_qualify_for_hoh`, `Filers.filer_could_qualify_for_qss`),
  read by DF's question gates and by `personQualifiesTPforHoH` / `...Qss`;
- `/filingStatus` (an input everywhere, CONVENTIONS section 7), for the
  knockout `/knockoutHohQpHasNoTin` only.

Not here, on purpose: choosing one HoH qualifying person among several (the
Writable `/hohQualifyingPerson`, a screen concern; out of scope per issue
#19), the filing-status eligibility itself (`/eligibleForHoh`,
`/eligibleForQss`, issue #20), and the filer-side tests of IRC 2(a)(2),
2(b)(1) "not married", 2(b)(2), 2(b)(3)(A) and 7703(b)(3) (`Filers`, #15).
This module feeds no Form 1040 dollar line directly; it feeds the filing
status (Form 1040 "Filing Status" checkboxes, and the HoH/QSS child's name in
the entry space when the child is not claimed).

The inputs are complete (CONVENTIONS section 3). Where DF disagrees with the
statute, the statute wins (CONVENTIONS section 8): four places below, marked
`# DF-BUG(19)` to `# DF-BUG(22)`.

## Declarations

```catala-metadata
## The `Writable` answers DF keeps per household member that only the HoH and
## QSS tests read.
declaration structure UpkeepAnswers:
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/tpPaidMostOfHomeUpkeep ("Did taxpayer pay more
  # than half the cost of keeping up the home shared with the dependent?")
  data tp_paid_most_of_home_upkeep content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/tpPaidMostOfParentHomeUpkeep ("Did taxpayer pay
  # more than half the cost of keeping up their dependent parent's main
  # home?")
  data tp_paid_most_of_parent_home_upkeep content boolean

## One member of DF /familyAndHousehold: the dependent answers (qualifying
## child, dependent and benefit-split answers, `Qualifying_relative.Member`)
## and the upkeep answers.
declaration structure Member:
  data dependent content R.Member
  data upkeep content UpkeepAnswers
```

## The scope for one person

```catala-metadata
declaration scope HohQssPersonTest:
  # One member of DF /familyAndHousehold
  input member content Member
  # DF: filers.xml /minimumFilerDateOfBirth (Filers.minimum_filer_date_of_birth)
  input minimum_filer_date_of_birth content date
  # DF: filers.xml /treatFilersAsDependents (Filers.treat_filers_as_dependents)
  input treat_filers_as_dependents content boolean
  # DF: filers.xml /eitherFilerUSCitizenOrNational
  input either_filer_us_citizen_or_national content boolean
  # DF: filingStatus.xml /treatAsMFJ (Filers.treat_as_mfj)
  input treat_as_mfj content boolean
  # DF: filingStatus.xml /eligibleForMFJ (Filers.eligible_for_mfj)
  input eligible_for_mfj content boolean
  # DF: filers.xml /filerCouldQualifyForHoh (Filers.filer_could_qualify_for_hoh)
  input filer_could_qualify_for_hoh content boolean
  # DF: filers.xml /filerCouldQualifyForQss (Filers.filer_could_qualify_for_qss)
  input filer_could_qualify_for_qss content boolean

  # The qualifying child, qualifying relative, dependent and benefit-split
  # tests for the same person (issues #16, #17, #18)
  output dep content R.DependentTest

  # DF: dependentsRelationship.xml /familyAndHousehold/*/marriedHohRelationshipTest
  output married_hoh_relationship_test condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/isBiologicalAdoptedFosterOrStepchild
  output is_biological_adopted_foster_or_stepchild condition
  # The person is the filer's father or mother (DF relationship `parent`; no
  # DF fact of its own, read by /hasHohQualifyingParent)
  output is_parent condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/qssPersonResidencyTest
  output qss_person_residency_test condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/flowAddPersonKeepingUpHome (a question gate
  # that DF ANDs into the third HoH branch; see below)
  output flow_add_person_keeping_up_home condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/flowAddPersonKeepingUpParentsHome (likewise,
  # second HoH branch)
  output flow_add_person_keeping_up_parents_home condition

  # IRC 7703(b)(1) for this person: a child the filer is entitled to claim
  # (or would be but for 152(e)). No DF fact; DF-BUG(19).
  output considered_unmarried_child condition
  # IRC 2(b)(1)(A)(i)(I)-(II): the child is not excluded as married. No DF
  # fact; DF-BUG(21).
  output married_child_not_excluded condition
  # The three branches of DF's /hohQualifyingPerson (no DF fact each; kept
  # apart so each branch sits under its own paragraph of IRC 2(b)(1))
  output hoh_qualifying_child_path condition
  output hoh_parent_path condition
  output hoh_other_relative_path condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/hohQualifyingPerson
  output hoh_qualifying_person condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/personQualifiesTPforHoH
  output person_qualifies_tp_for_hoh condition
  # DF: /hohQPHasTin, for this person (DF tests only the person the filer
  # chose; see the household scope)
  output hoh_qp_has_tin condition

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/qssQualifyingPerson
  output qss_qualifying_person condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/personQualifiesTPforQss
  output person_qualifies_tp_for_qss condition
```

```catala
scope HohQssPersonTest:
  definition dep equals
    output of R.DependentTest with {
      -- member: member.dependent
      -- minimum_filer_date_of_birth: minimum_filer_date_of_birth
      -- treat_filers_as_dependents: treat_filers_as_dependents
      -- either_filer_us_citizen_or_national: either_filer_us_citizen_or_national
      -- treat_as_mfj: treat_as_mfj }
```

## 26 U.S.C. § 2(b) Definition of head of household

### (1) In general

IRC 2(b)(1) (`sources/statute/irc-2.html`): "For purposes of this subtitle, an
individual shall be considered a head of a household if, and only if, such
individual is not married at the close of his taxable year, is not a
surviving spouse (as defined in subsection (a)), and either— (A) maintains as
his home a household which constitutes for more than one-half of such taxable
year the principal place of abode, as a member of such household, of— (i) a
qualifying child of the individual (as defined in section 152(c), determined
without regard to section 152(e)), but not if such child— (I) is married at
the close of the taxpayer's taxable year, and (II) is not a dependent of such
individual by reason of section 152(b)(2) or 152(b)(3), or both, or (ii) any
other person who is a dependent of the taxpayer, if the taxpayer is entitled
to a deduction for the taxable year for such person under section 151, or (B)
maintains a household which constitutes for such taxable year the principal
place of abode of the father or mother of the taxpayer, if the taxpayer is
entitled to a deduction for the taxable year for such father or mother under
section 151. For purposes of this paragraph, an individual shall be
considered as maintaining a household only if over half of the cost of
maintaining the household during the taxable year is furnished by such
individual."

Pub. 501 (2024), `sources/pubs/p501--2024.pdf` p 9, Head of Household: "You
may be able to file as head of household if you meet all the following
requirements. 1. You are unmarried or considered unmarried on the last day of
the year. ... 2. You paid more than half the cost of keeping up a home for
the year. 3. A qualifying person lived with you in the home for more than
half the year (except for temporary absences, such as school). However, if
the qualifying person is your dependent parent, your dependent parent doesn't
have to live with you." Treas. Reg. § 1.2-2(b)(2) (`sources/regs/cfr-1.2-2.xml`):
"Under no circumstances shall the same person be used to qualify more than
one taxpayer as the head of a household for the same taxable year."

"Over half of the cost of maintaining the household" is the answer
`tp_paid_most_of_home_upkeep` (Pub. 501 p 9, Worksheet 1, "Cost of Keeping Up
a Home"; Treas. Reg. § 1.7703-1(b)(4) lists the costs). Birth, death,
temporary absences, adoption and foster placement during the year (Pub. 501 p
9) are part of the residency answer, as in DF (`Qualifying_child`).

Pub. 501 p 10, Table 4, "Who Is a Qualifying Person Qualifying You To File as
Head of Household?", has three rows, one per branch below:
"qualifying child (such as a son, daughter, or grandchild who lived with you
more than half the year and meets certain other tests)"; "qualifying relative
who is your father or mother"; "qualifying relative other than your father or
mother (such as a grandparent, brother, or sister who meets certain tests)".

#### (A)(i) A qualifying child

Table 4, first row: "the child is single: a qualifying person, whether or not
the child meets the citizen or resident test. the child is married and you
can claim the child as a dependent: a qualifying person. the child is married
and you can't claim the child as a dependent: not a qualifying person."
Footnote 3: "This person is a qualifying person if the only reason you can't
claim the person as a dependent is that you can be claimed as a dependent on
another taxpayer's return." Footnote 1: "If you are the custodial parent and
those rules apply, the child is generally your qualifying child for head of
household filing status even though the child isn't a qualifying child you
can claim as a dependent."

The qualifying child is "determined without regard to section 152(e)": DF's
`qualifyingChild` is the plain 152(c) test (`Qualifying_child`), and the
benefit split only removes the child as a *dependent* (`Qualifying_relative`).
The custodial parent who released the claim keeps the child here.

DF's branch (lines 3355-3389): the person is a qualifying child, the filer
paid most of the home upkeep, the child is not the qualifying child of
another taxpayer unless the filer claims the child (DF's way of applying the
tie-breakers of IRC 152(c)(4), Q6 in CONVENTIONS section 8), and the child is
unmarried or is an "eligible dependent minus filer information" (or an
eligible dependent, or a split child who is unmarried, both covered by the
first two).

**The married child.** The statute excludes a married child only if the child
"is not a dependent of such individual by reason of section 152(b)(2) or
152(b)(3)", i.e. the child filed a joint return (152(b)(2), the joint return
test with its refund-only exception, `Qualifying_child.joint_return_test`) or
fails the citizen or resident test (152(b)(3), `citizen_or_resident_test`).
DF instead requires `eligibleDependentMinusFilerInformation`, which also
fails when the child has no TIN or when the filer, as custodial parent,
released the claim (152(e)). Neither is a reason under 152(b)(2) or (b)(3), so
the statute keeps those married children; Table 4 and footnote 1 agree. This
is `DF-BUG(21)`. (A qualifying child who filed a joint return fails 152(c)(1)(E)
already, so the first half of the exclusion only matters for clarity.)

```catala
scope HohQssPersonTest:
  # DF-BUG(21): DF excludes a married qualifying child unless
  # eligibleDependentMinusFilerInformation, which also needs a TIN and no
  # benefit split. The statute excludes a married child only for 152(b)(2) or
  # (b)(3). IRC 2(b)(1)(A)(i)(I)-(II); Pub 501 p 10 Table 4 and footnote 1.
  # Status: candidate. No DF fact.
  rule married_child_not_excluded under condition
    not member.dependent.person.married
    or (dep.qc.joint_return_test and dep.citizen_or_resident_test)
  consequence fulfilled

  # IRC 2(b)(1)(A)(i); Pub 501 pp 9-10, Table 4 row 1.
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/hohQualifyingPerson, first branch
  rule hoh_qualifying_child_path under condition
    dep.qualifying_child
    and member.upkeep.tp_paid_most_of_home_upkeep
    and (not dep.is_qualifying_child_of_another or dep.is_claimed_dependent)
    and married_child_not_excluded
  consequence fulfilled
```

#### (B) The father or mother

Pub. 501 p 9, Special rule for parent: "If your qualifying person is your
parent, you may be eligible to file as head of household even if your parent
doesn't live with you. However, you must be able to claim your parent as a
dependent. Also, you must pay more than half the cost of keeping up a home
that was the main home for the entire year for your parent. If you pay more
than half the cost of keeping your parent in a rest home or home for the
elderly, that counts as paying more than half the cost of keeping up your
parent's main home." Table 4, second row: "qualifying relative who is your
father or mother: you can claim your parent as a dependent: a qualifying
person." Treas. Reg. § 1.2-2(b)(4): "For this purpose, a person who legally
adopted the taxpayer is considered the father or mother of the taxpayer."

"Entitled to a deduction ... under section 151" is DF's `eligibleDependent`:
a dependent (152), the filer is nobody's dependent (152(b)(1)), and a TIN
(IRC 151(e): "No exemption shall be allowed under this section with respect
to any individual unless the TIN of such individual is included on the return
claiming the exemption.").

DF's branch (lines 3391-3404) asks the parent-home question
(`tpPaidMostOfParentHomeUpkeep`) only for a parent who lived with the filer
less than six months (`flowAddPersonKeepingUpParentsHome`, line 1959, which
also requires `/filerCouldQualifyForHoh`). A parent who lived with the filer
six months or more is a member of the filer's household and goes through the
third branch with the filer's own home upkeep. The gate is reproduced: it only
chooses which home's upkeep is asked about, and `/filerCouldQualifyForHoh` is
ANDed into `/eligibleForHoh` anyway (`filingStatus.xml` line 566).

Multiple support agreements (IRC 2(b)(3)(B)(ii), Table 4 footnote 5) are
part of the support answer `writableQrSupportTest` in DF; not modelled
(`Qualifying_relative`).

```catala
scope HohQssPersonTest:
  # IRC 2(b)(1)(B) "the father or mother"; Treas. Reg. 1.2-2(b)(4).
  # DF: dependentsRelationship.xml /relationshipOptions `parent`
  rule is_parent under condition
    member.dependent.person.relationship = Q.Relationship.Parent
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/flowAddPersonKeepingUpParentsHome
  rule flow_add_person_keeping_up_parents_home under condition
    filer_could_qualify_for_hoh
    and is_parent
    and member.dependent.person.residency_duration
          = Q.ResidencyDuration.LessThanSixMonths
  consequence fulfilled

  # IRC 2(b)(1)(B), 151(e); Treas. Reg. 1.2-2(b)(4); Pub 501 pp 9-10, Table 4
  # row 2. DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/hohQualifyingPerson, second branch
  rule hoh_parent_path under condition
    dep.qualifying_relative
    and dep.eligible_dependent
    and flow_add_person_keeping_up_parents_home
    and member.upkeep.tp_paid_most_of_parent_home_upkeep
    and is_parent
  consequence fulfilled
```

#### (A)(ii) Any other dependent, and (3)(B) the limitations

IRC 2(b)(3): "Notwithstanding paragraph (1), for purposes of this subtitle a
taxpayer shall not be considered to be a head of a household— ... (B) by
reason of an individual who would not be a dependent for the taxable year but
for— (i) subparagraph (H) of section 152(d)(2), or (ii) paragraph (3) of
section 152(d)."

Table 4, third row: "qualifying relative other than your father or mother
(such as a grandparent, brother, or sister who meets certain tests): your
relative lived with you more than half the year, and your relative is related
to you in one of the ways listed under Relatives who don't have to live with
you, later, and you can claim your relative as a dependent: a qualifying
person. ... your relative isn't related to you in one of the ways listed ...
and is your qualifying relative only because your relative lived with you all
year as a member of your household: not a qualifying person." Pub. 501 p 9,
Example 3, your friend: "your friend isn't your qualifying person for head of
household purposes because your friend isn't related to you in one of the
ways listed under Relatives who don't have to live with you".

DF's branch (lines 3405-3424): a qualifying relative, related by 152(d)(2)(A)
to (G) (`qrRelationshipTest`, so not by (H) alone), who lived with the filer
more than half the year (`residencyTest`), the filer paid most of the home
upkeep, and the person is an eligible dependent **or** the filer can claim the
person for HoH through a benefit split (`tpCanClaimSplitBenefitsHohEitcCdcc`).
The gate `flowAddPersonKeepingUpHome` (line 1903) is reproduced: given the
other conditions of this branch it reduces to `/filerCouldQualifyForHoh` or
`/filerCouldQualifyForQss` (its other disjunct, an MFS filer considered
unmarried for the CDCC, implies `/filerCouldQualifyForHoh`: `filers.xml`,
`Filers.mfs_filer_could_be_considered_unmarried_for_cdcc` needs
`lived_apart_last_six_months`), and `/eligibleForHoh` ANDs
`/filerCouldQualifyForHoh` in, so the gate changes no filing status.

**The benefit split.** 2(b)(1)(A)(ii) requires that "the taxpayer is
entitled to a deduction for the taxable year for such person under section
151". Only the qualifying child of (A)(i) is "determined without regard to
section 152(e)", and Pub. 501 footnote 1 keeps only the custodial parent's
*qualifying child*. A person who is the filer's qualifying relative (and,
since `DF-BUG(15)`, not the filer's qualifying child) on the custodial side
of a split is not a dependent the filer may claim, so not a qualifying person
here. DF admits them. The reachable cases are rare: a child under 18 who
filed a joint return (no qualifying child, 152(c)(1)(E)), or, on the
non-parent path, a minor who is older than the filer (152(c)(3)(A)). This is
`DF-BUG(22)`.

**DF-BUG(15) and this branch (issue #17's question).** DF's
`qualifyingRelative` can be true for the filer's own qualifying child. The
only configuration where this third branch then admits a qualifying child
that the first branch rejects is a married qualifying child on the custodial
side of a benefit split who also passes the qualifying relative gross income
and support tests: the first branch rejects them through
`eligibleDependentMinusFilerInformation` (false under a split) and the third
admits them through `tpCanClaimSplitBenefitsHohEitcCdcc`. Here the third
branch rejects them (not a qualifying relative, DF-BUG(15)) and the first
admits them (DF-BUG(21), whatever their gross income and support). Every
other qualifying child the third branch admits in DF is admitted by the first
branch too. So the HoH result is unaffected by DF-BUG(15).

```catala
scope HohQssPersonTest:
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/flowAddPersonKeepingUpHome. The CDCC disjunct
  # (/mfsFilerCouldBeConsiderUnmarriedForCdcc and cdccQualifyingPerson)
  # implies /filerCouldQualifyForHoh (text above), so it is left out.
  rule flow_add_person_keeping_up_home under condition
    (filer_could_qualify_for_hoh or filer_could_qualify_for_qss)
    and (dep.qc.qc_relationship_test
         or (dep.qr_relationship_test
             and member.dependent.person.residency_duration
                   != Q.ResidencyDuration.LessThanSixMonths))
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF-BUG(22): DF also admits a qualifying relative the filer cannot claim
  # because of a benefit split (tpCanClaimSplitBenefitsHohEitcCdcc). IRC
  # 2(b)(1)(A)(ii) needs a dependent the filer is entitled to claim. Status:
  # candidate.
  # IRC 2(b)(1)(A)(ii), 2(b)(3)(B)(i); Pub 501 pp 9-10, Table 4 row 3,
  # Example 3. DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/hohQualifyingPerson, third branch
  rule hoh_other_relative_path under condition
    dep.qualifying_relative
    and dep.eligible_dependent
    and dep.qr_relationship_test
    and dep.qc.residency_test
    and member.upkeep.tp_paid_most_of_home_upkeep
    and flow_add_person_keeping_up_home
  consequence fulfilled
```

### (2) and § 7703(b): married individuals considered unmarried

IRC 2(c): "For purposes of this part, an individual shall be treated as not
married at the close of the taxable year if such individual is so treated
under the provisions of section 7703(b)." IRC 7703(b)
(`sources/statute/irc-7703.html`): "if— (1) an individual who is married
(within the meaning of subsection (a)) and who files a separate return
maintains as his home a household which constitutes for more than one-half
of the taxable year the principal place of abode of a child (within the
meaning of section 152(f)(1)) with respect to whom such individual is
entitled to a deduction for the taxable year under section 151 (or would be
so entitled but for section 152(e)), (2) such individual furnishes over
one-half of the cost of maintaining such household during the taxable year,
and (3) during the last 6 months of the taxable year, such individual's
spouse is not a member of such household, such individual shall not be
considered as married." IRC 152(f)(1): "(A) ... a son, daughter, stepson, or
stepdaughter of the taxpayer, or (ii) an eligible foster child of the
taxpayer. (B) ... a legally adopted individual of the taxpayer ... shall be
treated as a child of such individual by blood."

Pub. 501 p 9, Considered Unmarried: "You are considered unmarried on the last
day of the tax year if you meet all the following tests. 1. You file a
separate return. ... 2. You paid more than half the cost of keeping up your
home for the tax year. 3. Your spouse didn't live in your home during the
last 6 months of the tax year. ... 4. Your home was the main home of your
child, stepchild, or foster child for more than half the year. ... 5. You
must be able to claim the child as a dependent. However, you meet this test
if you can't claim the child as a dependent only because the noncustodial
parent can claim the child using the rules described later in Children of
divorced or separated parents".

DF (line 3339): if the filer is `/eligibleForMFJ` (married, spouse not a
nonresident alien), the qualifying person must pass
`marriedHohRelationshipTest` (biological, adopted, step or foster child). The
filer-side tests 1 and 3 are `Filers` (#15: `/filerCouldQualifyForHoh` needs
`/livedApartLastSixMonths`); tests 2 and 4 are in every branch above
(upkeep, more than half the year). DF does not check test 5: a child who is
the filer's qualifying child but whom the filer cannot claim (the filer is
someone's dependent, 152(b)(1); the child fails the citizen or resident test;
the child has no TIN, 151(e)) passes the first branch when unmarried, so DF
treats the married filer as unmarried. The statute does not: the child must
be one "with respect to whom such individual is entitled to a deduction ...
(or would be so entitled but for section 152(e))". This is `DF-BUG(19)`.

Applying the restriction to the qualifying person rather than to the filer is
DF's (the statute makes the filer unmarried through a 7703(b) child, after
which any qualifying person counts). The household result is the same: a
7703(b) child is always a qualifying person itself (first or third branch).

```catala
scope HohQssPersonTest:
  # IRC 7703(b)(1), 152(f)(1). DF: dependentsRelationship.xml
  # /familyAndHousehold/*/marriedHohRelationshipTest
  rule married_hoh_relationship_test under condition
    match member.dependent.person.relationship with pattern
    -- Q.Relationship.BiologicalChild: true
    -- Q.Relationship.AdoptedChild: true
    -- Q.Relationship.StepChild: true
    -- Q.Relationship.FosterChild: true
    -- anything: false
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/isBiologicalAdoptedFosterOrStepchild (same test
  # as marriedHohRelationshipTest; DF keeps both, read by /hasHohQualifyingChild)
  rule is_biological_adopted_foster_or_stepchild under condition
    married_hoh_relationship_test
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF-BUG(19): DF only checks the relationship. IRC 7703(b)(1) also needs a
  # child "with respect to whom such individual is entitled to a deduction
  # ... under section 151 (or would be so entitled but for section 152(e))";
  # Pub 501 p 9, Considered Unmarried, test 5. Status: candidate. No DF fact.
  rule considered_unmarried_child under condition
    married_hoh_relationship_test
    and (dep.eligible_dependent
         or dep.split.tp_can_claim_split_benefits_hoh_eitc_cdcc)
  consequence fulfilled
```

### The qualifying person

DF `/familyAndHousehold/*/hohQualifyingPerson` (line 3332) is the `All` of:
the married-filer restriction above, not `disqualifiedDueToParentalCustody`
(a non-parent who reached a benefit split but loses the child to the parents
under the tie-breaker of IRC 152(c)(4)(C), `Dependents_benefit_split`), the
person `isClaimedIfClaimable`, and one of the three branches.

**Claimed if claimable.** DF requires that a person the filer can claim as a
dependent is claimed (`isClaimedIfClaimable`; its description cites an
internal decision record, "Must claim dependent on return ... a claimable
dependent must be claimed to act as a QP in TY2023"). IRC 2(b)(1) asks for a
qualifying child (no deduction at all) or a person for whom "the taxpayer is
entitled to a deduction", not one the taxpayer claims; Pub. 501 p 9, How to
file: "If the child who qualifies you for this filing status isn't claimed as
your dependent in the Dependents section of Form 1040 or 1040-SR, enter the
child's name in the entry space at the bottom of the Filing Status section."
A filer who chooses not to list an eligible dependent keeps head of
household. This is `DF-BUG(20)`; it applies to QSS as well (below).

```catala
scope HohQssPersonTest:
  # DF-BUG(19) (considered_unmarried_child replaces
  # marriedHohRelationshipTest) and DF-BUG(20) (no isClaimedIfClaimable):
  # IRC 2(b)(1), 2(c), 7703(b)(1); Pub 501 p 9 "How to file". Status:
  # candidate. IRC 2(b)(1); Pub 501 pp 9-10.
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/hohQualifyingPerson
  rule hoh_qualifying_person under condition
    (not eligible_for_mfj or considered_unmarried_child)
    and not dep.split.disqualified_due_to_parental_custody
    and (hoh_qualifying_child_path or hoh_parent_path or hoh_other_relative_path)
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/personQualifiesTPforHoH
  rule person_qualifies_tp_for_hoh under condition
    hoh_qualifying_person and filer_could_qualify_for_hoh
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF: /hohQPHasTin (for this person)
  rule hoh_qp_has_tin under condition
    member.dependent.answers.tin_type != R.TinType.NoTin
  consequence fulfilled
```

## 26 U.S.C. § 2(a) Definition of surviving spouse

IRC 2(a)(1): "the term "surviving spouse" means a taxpayer— (A) whose spouse
died during either of his two taxable years immediately preceding the taxable
year, and (B) who maintains as his home a household which constitutes for the
taxable year the principal place of abode (as a member of such household) of
a dependent (i) who (within the meaning of section 152, determined without
regard to subsections (b)(1), (b)(2), and (d)(1)(B) thereof) is a son,
stepson, daughter, or stepdaughter of the taxpayer, and (ii) with respect to
whom the taxpayer is entitled to a deduction for the taxable year under
section 151. For purposes of this paragraph, an individual shall be
considered as maintaining a household only if over half of the cost of
maintaining the household during the taxable year is furnished by such
individual." Treas. Reg. § 1.2-2(a)(1)(ii): "a person who is (whether by blood
or adoption) a son, stepson, daughter, or stepdaughter of the taxpayer".

Pub. 501 p 10, Qualifying Surviving Spouse, Eligibility rules: "3. You have a
child or stepchild (not a foster child) whom you can claim as a dependent or
could claim as a dependent except that, for 2024: a. The child had gross
income of $5,050 or more, b. The child filed a joint return, or c. You could
be claimed as a dependent on someone else's return. ... 4. This child lived in
your home all year, except for temporary absences. ... 5. You paid more than
half the cost of keeping up a home for the year." Pub. 501 p 11, Death or
birth: "You must have provided more than half of the cost of keeping up a
home that was the child's main home during the entire part of the year the
child was alive." Adopted child: "The child is considered to have lived with
you for all of 2024 if your main home was this child's main home for the
entire time since this child was adopted or placed with you in 2024."

The filer-side tests (2(a)(1)(A), (2)) are `Filers.filer_could_qualify_for_qss`
(#15).

DF `/familyAndHousehold/*/qssQualifyingPerson` (line 3443): the filer paid
most of the home upkeep; the person is a biological, adopted or step child
(not a foster child); the citizen or resident test (152(b)(3) is not among
the disregarded subsections); lived with the filer all year
(`qssPersonResidencyTest`); and either the qualifying child age and support
tests or the qualifying relative "not a qualifying child of another" and
support tests, without the joint return test, the gross income test and the
filer's dependency (the three disregarded subsections and 152(c)(1)(E), as
Pub. 501 test 3 says); and the filer is not the custodial parent who released
the claim (`tpCanClaimSplitBenefitsHohEitcCdcc`: then the filer is not
"entitled to a deduction", 2(a)(1)(B)(ii), and 2(a) has no "but for 152(e)").
DF also requires `isClaimedIfClaimable`: `DF-BUG(20)`, as for HoH ("whom you
can claim as a dependent", not "whom you claim").

The qualifying relative half reads `notQualifyingChildTest`, which here
applies the answer "qualifying child of another taxpayer" to every person who
can be anyone's qualifying child (`Qualifying_relative`, DF-BUG(16)).

```catala
scope HohQssPersonTest:
  # IRC 2(a)(1)(B); Pub 501 p 10 test 4. DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/qssPersonResidencyTest
  rule qss_person_residency_test under condition
    member.dependent.person.residency_duration = Q.ResidencyDuration.AllYear
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF-BUG(20): DF also requires isClaimedIfClaimable. Status: candidate.
  # IRC 2(a)(1)(B), 152(f)(1)(B); Treas. Reg. 1.2-2(a)(1)(ii); Pub 501 pp
  # 10-11. DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/qssQualifyingPerson
  rule qss_qualifying_person under condition
    member.upkeep.tp_paid_most_of_home_upkeep
    and (member.dependent.person.relationship = Q.Relationship.BiologicalChild
         or dep.child_is_adopted
         or member.dependent.person.relationship = Q.Relationship.StepChild)
    and dep.citizen_or_resident_test
    and qss_person_residency_test
    and ((dep.qc.age_test and dep.qc.qc_support_test)
         or (dep.not_qualifying_child_test and dep.qr_support_test))
    and not dep.split.tp_can_claim_split_benefits_hoh_eitc_cdcc
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/personQualifiesTPforQss
  rule person_qualifies_tp_for_qss under condition
    qss_qualifying_person and filer_could_qualify_for_qss
  consequence fulfilled
```

## The household: every person

The scope `HohQssPersons` applies `HohQssPersonTest` to each member of
`/familyAndHousehold`, in order, and counts the qualifying persons. The counts
are what `filingStatus.xml` reads: `/eligibleForHoh` (line 549) is "the Count
of `/familyAndHousehold/*/hohQualifyingPerson` is greater than 0" and
`/filerCouldQualifyForHoh` and not `/eligibleForQss`; `/eligibleForQss` (line
576) is `/filerCouldQualifyForQss` and "the Count of
`/familyAndHousehold/*/qssQualifyingPerson` is greater than 0" (issue #20).

**No TIN.** DF `/knockoutHohQpHasNoTin` (line 5107, `flow.xml` line 66): the
filer files as head of household and the qualifying person the filer chose
(`/hohQualifyingPerson`, a Writable collection item) has no TIN. IRC 2(b)
asks for no TIN for a qualifying child who is not claimed (Pub. 501 p 9 asks
only for the child's name); a qualifying parent or other relative needs one
anyway (151(e), through `eligibleDependent`). Refusing the return is a scope
decision of DF, reproduced as a knockout (CONVENTIONS section 6). The choice
among several qualifying persons is out of scope (issue #19), so this module
assumes the filer chooses one with a TIN when there is one: the knockout
fires when the filer files as head of household, has at least one qualifying
person, and none of them has a TIN. With no qualifying person at all, the
filing status is not eligible (#20's knockout), not this one.

```catala-metadata
declaration scope HohQssPersons:
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold
  input members content list of Member
  # DF: filers.xml /minimumFilerDateOfBirth
  input minimum_filer_date_of_birth content date
  # DF: filers.xml /treatFilersAsDependents
  input treat_filers_as_dependents content boolean
  # DF: filers.xml /eitherFilerUSCitizenOrNational
  input either_filer_us_citizen_or_national content boolean
  # DF: filingStatus.xml /treatAsMFJ
  input treat_as_mfj content boolean
  # DF: filingStatus.xml /eligibleForMFJ
  input eligible_for_mfj content boolean
  # DF: filers.xml /filerCouldQualifyForHoh
  input filer_could_qualify_for_hoh content boolean
  # DF: filers.xml /filerCouldQualifyForQss
  input filer_could_qualify_for_qss content boolean
  # DF: filingStatus.xml /filingStatus
  input filing_status content Types.FilingStatus

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/... (one result per member, same order)
  output results content list of HohQssPersonTest
  # DF: Count(/familyAndHousehold/*/hohQualifyingPerson), read by /eligibleForHoh
  output hoh_qualifying_people_count content integer
  # DF: Count(/familyAndHousehold/*/qssQualifyingPerson), read by /eligibleForQss
  output qss_qualifying_people_count content integer
  # DF: /hasHohQualifyingChild
  output has_hoh_qualifying_child condition
  # DF: /hasHohQualifyingParent
  output has_hoh_qualifying_parent condition
  # HoH qualifying persons with a TIN (no DF fact; text above)
  output hoh_qualifying_people_with_tin_count content integer
  # DF: /knockoutHohQpHasNoTin
  output ko_knockout_hoh_qp_has_no_tin condition
  # CONVENTIONS section 6: every ko_ of this scope
  output unsupported condition
```

```catala
scope HohQssPersons:
  definition results equals
    map each m among members to
      (output of HohQssPersonTest with {
         -- member: m
         -- minimum_filer_date_of_birth: minimum_filer_date_of_birth
         -- treat_filers_as_dependents: treat_filers_as_dependents
         -- either_filer_us_citizen_or_national:
              either_filer_us_citizen_or_national
         -- treat_as_mfj: treat_as_mfj
         -- eligible_for_mfj: eligible_for_mfj
         -- filer_could_qualify_for_hoh: filer_could_qualify_for_hoh
         -- filer_could_qualify_for_qss: filer_could_qualify_for_qss })

  # filingStatus.xml /eligibleForHoh, the Count
  definition hoh_qualifying_people_count equals
    number of (list of r among results such that r.hoh_qualifying_person)

  # filingStatus.xml /eligibleForQss, the Count
  definition qss_qualifying_people_count equals
    number of (list of r among results such that r.qss_qualifying_person)

  # DF: /hasHohQualifyingChild
  rule has_hoh_qualifying_child under condition
    exists r among results such that
      r.hoh_qualifying_person and r.is_biological_adopted_foster_or_stepchild
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF: /hasHohQualifyingParent
  rule has_hoh_qualifying_parent under condition
    exists r among results such that
      r.hoh_qualifying_person and r.is_parent
  consequence fulfilled

  definition hoh_qualifying_people_with_tin_count equals
    number of (list of r among results such that
                 r.hoh_qualifying_person and r.hoh_qp_has_tin)

  # DF: /knockoutHohQpHasNoTin, with the filer choosing a person with a TIN
  # when there is one (text above)
  rule ko_knockout_hoh_qp_has_no_tin under condition
    filing_status = Types.FilingStatus.HeadOfHousehold
    and hoh_qualifying_people_count > 0
    and hoh_qualifying_people_with_tin_count = 0
  consequence fulfilled

  # flow.xml line 66
  rule unsupported under condition ko_knockout_hoh_qp_has_no_tin
  consequence fulfilled
```
